<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="hawaii-ws-measuringTape">
  <title>Let's Build a Measuring Tape</title>
  <page>
    <sidebyside widths="46% 20% 20%" margins="0% 5%">
      <p>We will first analyze a measuring tape that relates inches and centimeters. We will then build a measuring tape that relates kīko'o and poho.</p>
      <image source="hawaii-kikoo-basic.png">
        <shortdescription>A spread hand illustrating the kīko'o unit — distance across an open hand from thumb tip to pinky tip.</shortdescription>
      </image>
      <image source="hawaii-poho-basic.png">
        <shortdescription>A raised palm illustrating the poho unit — width of the palm.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </sidebyside>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-read-the-tape">
      <statement>
        <p>Use the tape with marks for <alert>in</alert> and <alert>cm</alert> to answer the questions.</p>
        <image source="hawaii-rulerinchcm.png" width="85%">
          <shortdescription>A ruler showing both inches (top, with fraction markings) and centimeters (bottom), used as a reference for reading a dual-scale measuring tape.</shortdescription>
        </image>
        <p>
        <ol>
          <li>If an object is <m>4.5</m> in long, how many cm is that? <fillin characters="10"/></li>
          <li><m>6.35</m> cm is how many inches? <fillin characters="10"/></li>
        </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      
      <solution>
        <p>Sample responses (looking at the ruler):</p>
        <ul>
          <li> <m>11.43</m> cm (or some close value)</li>
          <li> <m>2.5</m> inches </li>
        </ul>
      </solution>
      
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-build-measuring-tape">
      <statement>
        <p>Now build a single measuring tape for <alert>kīko'o</alert> and <alert>poho</alert>.</p>

                    <p>Key principles for building a measuring tape:
        <ul>
          <li>We start at zero (0).</li>
          <li>Numbers go from left to right.</li>
          <li>Label each time to identify the measure being marked.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>
        <sidebyside widths="60% 35%" margins="0% 5%">
          
          <p>Instructions:
            <ul>
              <li>Use the roll (one side) and markers.</li>
              <li>Whose hands will be used?</li>
              <li>Use an edge for kīko'o, the other for poho.</li>
              <li>Draw a 0 mark for both units. Then: 1, 2, 3, …</li>
              <li>No gaps! No overlaps!</li>
            </ul>
          </p>
          
          <image source="paper-roll.png" width="60%" decorative="yes"/>

        </sidebyside>
        <p>Answer the following questions:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>If an object is 4.5 kīko'o long, how many poho is that? <fillin characters="10"/></li>
          <li>5 poho is equal to how many kīko'o? <fillin characters="10"/></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    
  </page>
  
</worksheet>
